Earth has a magnetic field, as if a giant bar magnet were buried inside it. This field, along with our [[atmosphere|atmosphere]], shields us from most of the Sun's particles. [[@earth-magnet|Earth's magnetic field]] steers many of them towards the poles.

When they crash into gases high in the atmosphere, they make the air glow. That is the [[@aurora|aurora]], the northern and southern lights. Oxygen glows green and red, and nitrogen adds blue and purple. In a strong storm, auroras spread much farther from the poles than usual.

{{story:aurora}}

Landing on a carrier is one of the hardest things a pilot can do. The deck is small, it moves forward as the ship sails, and in rough seas it rises and falls. At night, it is a dim rectangle in a black ocean.

To help, carriers have a landing deck angled to one side. If a plane misses the wires, it can fly off the side without hitting parked aircraft at the front. A row of special lights shows the pilot whether the plane is too high or too low. An officer on deck, watching every landing, talks to the pilot by radio and can order them to go round again.

{{story:floating-ships}}

Conduction and convection both need particles, such as air, water or metal, to carry the heat. A vacuum has almost none. So heat from the inner wall has very little to pass through on its way to the outer wall.

That leaves radiation, which can cross empty space. It is how sunlight reaches Earth. To stop it, flask makers coat the walls with a shiny layer, like a mirror for heat. Infrared rays hit it and bounce back into the tea.

{{story:tea}}

The painters, called Nakashis, start with khadi cotton cloth. They coat it several times with a paste of rice [[starch|starch]], white clay, boiled tamarind seeds and gum. Each coat dries first. Then they sketch the outlines straight onto the cloth.

Traditionally, every colour came from nature. White came from crushed seashells, black from lamp soot and yellow from [[@turmeric|turmeric]]. Brushes were made from squirrel hair tied to sticks. Many painters now buy ready-made paints, but the method of building up layers is the same. One scroll can take weeks or months. Many scrolls open with a panel of Ganesha, painted to bless the story before it begins, often followed by Saraswati, the goddess of learning. Then the community's own heroes take over, sometimes for dozens of scenes.

{{story:storytelling-india}}

Indus cities and towns spread across today's Pakistan and north-west India, from Afghanistan to Gujarat and Haryana. Over a thousand sites are now known. Rakhigarhi in Haryana is one of the largest.

Dholavira, on an island in the [[@gujarat-rann|Rann of Kutch]] in Gujarat, had giant stone [[reservoir|reservoirs]] to catch rare rainwater. In 2021 it became a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Lothal, also in Gujarat, has a large brick basin that many experts think was a dock for boats.

{{story:toilets-sewers}}

Boa Sr lived on the Andaman Islands and was the last speaker of Bo, one of the Great Andamanese languages. Some linguists believe the Andamanese languages descend from some of the oldest languages in Asia. For her last years she had nobody to speak Bo with. She talked to birds, she told researchers, because they understood.

She survived the 2004 [[tsunami|tsunami]] and died in 2010, aged about 85. Researchers had recorded some of her speech and songs. Those recordings are now among the only traces of the language.

{{story:endangered-languages}}

Wimbledon is the oldest. It was first held in 1877 at a croquet club that had just added lawn tennis. The US championships began in 1881, the French in 1891 (open to foreigners from 1925) and the Australian in 1905.

In bridge, a card game, a "grand slam" means winning every trick in a hand. In 1933 an American sportswriter borrowed the phrase for the Australian player Jack Crawford, who had won three of the four big titles that year and nearly won the fourth.

{{story:sports-science}}

The retina has two kinds of light sensors. About 120 million *rods* in each eye work in dim light but can't see colour. That's why a garden at night looks grey. About 6 million *cones* work in brighter light and let you see [[@eyes-colour|colours]] and fine detail.

Cones are packed tightly in a small spot in the centre of the retina. Whatever you look straight at lands there, which is why the middle of your view is sharpest.

{{story:cameras-how}}

In the early 1990s, NASA scientists studied pilots on long night flights across the Pacific. One group was allowed a 40-minute rest in their seats, taking turns while the other pilot flew. The pilots who rested slept for about 26 minutes on average.

Later in the flight, those pilots reacted faster and had far fewer moments of drowsiness than pilots who stayed awake the whole time. The study, published in 1994, helped some airlines introduce planned rest for pilots, always with another pilot awake at the controls.

