The story goes back to 1824, when a 15-year-old French boy named Louis Braille invented a system of raised dots that blind people could read with their fingertips. Braille is still used today, on lift buttons, medicine boxes and Indian banknotes, which have raised marks to help blind people tell them apart.

{{story:braille}}

In 1976 the American inventor Ray Kurzweil built a reading machine that could [[@scanners|scan a printed page]] and read it aloud in a computer voice. It was as big as a washing machine. Today, a free phone app can do the same with the camera, reading menus, signs and even [[@words-handwriting|handwriting]].

The hard part is not sensing movement. It's deciding which movements are steps. Engineers record thousands of people walking, running, climbing stairs and doing other things, then tune the rules until the count matches what really happened.

Even so, no watch is perfect. Pushing a pram or a shopping trolley keeps your wrist still, so steps get missed. A bumpy ride or clapping along to music can add a few that never happened. Tests usually find good watches land within a few per cent of the real count when you walk normally, and drift further off during odd activities.

